\f1\b\fs60 \AppleTypeServices\AppleTypeServicesF65539 \cf2 \cb1 Keeping Your Cool While Your Kid Melts Down\
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\f2\i\b0\fs24 \AppleTypeServices \cf4 \strokec4 A short article for parents\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sa213\partightenfactor0

\f3\i0\fs32 \cf0 \outl0\strokewidth0 We\'92ve all been there: you\'92re in the middle of a grocery aisle, just trying to put on shoes, or walking into your home from work and suddenly your child hits the "red zone." And, let\'92s face it, this is not just for young children. All children have moments when the demands of their day get the best of them. When the whining and crying starts, the flailing or slamming doors begins, you feel your own heart rate start to climb
\fs26\fsmilli13333 \

\fs32 When your child is melting down, your brain\'92s natural "fight or flight" response kicks in. But here\'92s the secret: you cannot settle a storm if you become the storm. Here is how to stay grounded when the chaos hits.
\fs26\fsmilli13333 \

\f4\b\fs32 The "Pause and Breathe" Rule
\f3\b0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \

\fs32 Before you react, take one deep, conscious breath. This isn't just "yoga talk", it\'92s physiology. A deep breath signals to your nervous system that there is no actual physical danger, preventing you from flipping into "angry mode." If you can breathe deeply, your body knows you are not trying to run away from a ferocious tiger who wants to eat you for dinner.
\fs26\fsmilli13333 \

\f4\b\fs32 Remind Yourself: It\'92s Not a Power Struggle
\f3\b0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \

\fs32 It is helpful to reframe the situation. Your child isn't 
\f5\i giving
\f3\i0  you a hard time; they are 
\f5\i having
\f3\i0  a hard time. At that moment, their brain\'92s "downstairs" (the emotional center) has completely taken over their "upstairs" (the logical center). They literally cannot be reasonable right now. And, most of what you say, they will not be able to hear.
\fs26\fsmilli13333 \

\f4\b\fs32 Get Low and Go Slow.
\f3\b0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \

\fs32 When we get stressed, we tend to tower over our kids and raise our voices. Try the opposite:
\fs26\fsmilli13333 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa213\partightenfactor0
\ls1\ilvl0
\f4\b\fs32 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Get low:
\f3\b0  Squat down to their eye level or sit on the floor nearby.
\fs26\fsmilli13333 \
\ls1\ilvl0
\fs32 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
B
\f4\b reathe in deeply again
\f3\b0 .
\f4\b  
\f3\b0 Let your child see you calming down yourself.
\fs26\fsmilli13333 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa213\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0
\f4\b\fs32 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Lower your volume:
\f3\b0  The quieter and slower you speak, the more they have to quiet down to hear you. You might even try to use a whisper using one or two words.
\fs26\fsmilli13333 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa213\partightenfactor0

\f4\b\fs32 \cf0 Use a Mental Mantra
\f3\b0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \

\fs32 Have a "reset phrase" ready to go in your head. Repeating a simple sentence can block out the noise and keep you focused. Practice this phrase when you are calm.
\fs26\fsmilli13333 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa213\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0
\fs32 \cf0 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
"This is not an emergency."
\fs26\fsmilli13333 \
\ls3\ilvl0
\fs32 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
"I am the calm anchor in this storm."
\fs26\fsmilli13333 \
\ls3\ilvl0\kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
\'93
\fs32 My response will either fuel the fire or calm it down.\'94\
\pard\tx720\pardeftab720\sa213\partightenfactor0

\fs26\fsmilli13333 \cf0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa213\partightenfactor0

\f4\b\fs32 \cf0 Postpone the "Lesson"
\f3\b0\fs26\fsmilli13333 \

\fs32 A meltdown is the worst time to teach a lesson or explain why they can\'92t have the blue cup or why your daughter cannot leave the house in what she is wearing. Save the discipline and the "talk" for 20 minutes later when everyone is regulated. Your only job during the meltdown is to provide a safe space for the feelings to pass.
\fs26\fsmilli13333 \

\fs32 You\'92re doing great. Parenting is intense, and staying calm is a skill that takes practice, not a switch you flip perfectly every time. If you do lose your cool? Don't beat yourself up, just apologize later and show them how to "repair" a relationship.
\fs26\fsmilli13333 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

